# Target C-II — FREE reflection blockers lie outside the force hull, so the linear inactive chain is an outer-face corridor

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only strengthening of the reflection inactive-chain reduction  
**Scope:** FREE reflection modules under C65 full visibility  
**Dependencies:** C65 full diameter visibility; canonical force hull `F=conv B`; FREE-module active-free blocker theorem; linear inactive-chain theorem; inactive-chain face localization  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_free_blockers_outer_face_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The previous face-localization theorem allowed the reflection chain to lie either in a bounded exceptional contact face or in the outer contact face. The bounded alternative is actually impossible for a FREE module.

Every FREE upper blocker lies strictly outside the force hull

\[
F=\operatorname{conv}B,
\]

where `B` is the complete actual-diameter endpoint set. The proof is one line of convexity: if a blocker `z` with `|z-q|>D` belonged to `F`, then some loaded vertex of `F` would be at least as far from `q`, creating a loaded blocker, forbidden by the FREE condition.

Therefore the root of the linear inactive chain starts outside `F`, hence in the outer face of the nonisolated contact graph. Since every chain edge is shorter than `sqrt(3)` and cannot cross a unit contact edge, the **entire** linear chain stays in that same outer face.

For every fixed

\[
0<\lambda<\frac12,
\]

a non-anchored FREE module thus forces an original fully inactive outer-face corridor of length

\[
\boxed{
\left({\lambda\over1+h_*}-o(1)\right)D
}
\]

whose support-depth span is

\[
\boxed{\lambda D-O(\sqrt D),}
\]

with every consecutive original distance in

\[
(1,1.3347479843\ldots+o(1)).
\]

This removes the bounded-exceptional-face escape from the reflection branch and couples the survivor directly to the canonical outer-face / outer-walk geometry.

---

## 1. No FREE blocker lies in the force hull

Let `G` be the unique nonisolated positive contact core and let

\[
F=\operatorname{conv}V(G)=\operatorname{conv}B,
\tag{OF.1}
\]

where C65 makes `B` the complete set of actual source-diameter endpoints.

Fix a FREE module with reflected apex `q` and blocker set

\[
U=\{z\in A:|z-q|>D\}.
\]

The FREE theorem gives

\[
\deg_C(z)=\deg_H(z)=0
\qquad(z\in U).
\tag{OF.2}
\]

Suppose, for contradiction, that some

\[
z\in U\cap F.
\]

Because `F=conv B`, write

\[
z=\sum_{b\in B}\mu_b b,
\qquad
\mu_b\ge0,
\qquad
\sum_b\mu_b=1.
\]

Convexity of the Euclidean norm gives

\[
|z-q|
\le\sum_b\mu_b|b-q|
\le\max_{b\in B}|b-q|.
\tag{OF.3}
\]

Since `|z-q|>D`, there exists

\[
b\in B
\]

with

\[
|b-q|>D.
\]

Thus `b in U`. But `b in B` is an actual diameter endpoint, so

\[
\deg_H(b)>0,
\]

contradicting (OF.2).

Hence

\[
\boxed{U\cap F=\varnothing.}
\tag{OF.4}

This applies to every blocker, not only the canonical farthest head.

---

## 2. Every blocker root is in the outer contact face

Under C-I/C65, the nonisolated unit-contact graph is exactly the positive contact core `G`; all points outside `G` are isolated vertices of the full contact graph.

The straight-line embedding of `G` is planar. Its convex hull is `F`. Any point strictly outside `F` lies in the unbounded face of this embedding.

By (OF.4), every blocker root

\[
z_0\in U
\]

of the absorption parent forest lies outside `F`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{z_0\text{ lies in the outer face of }G.}
\tag{OF.5}

---

## 3. The whole linear chain remains in the outer face

The linear inactive-chain theorem produces

\[
z_0,z_1,\ldots,z_\ell,
\]

where `z_0 in U` and every parent edge satisfies

\[
|z_j-z_{j+1}|
<1+h_*+o(1)
<\sqrt3.
\tag{OF.6}

The face-localization theorem proves that such an edge cannot cross any unit contact edge and cannot pass through a contact vertex. Therefore all chain vertices lie in one contact face.

The root face is the outer face by (OF.5). Hence

\[
\boxed{
\{z_0,\ldots,z_\ell\}
\text{ and every parent segment lie in the outer contact face.}
}
\tag{OF.7}

The bounded exceptional-face alternative from the preceding face-localization theorem is absent in the FREE reflection setting.

---

## 4. Macroscopic outer-face corridor

For every fixed `lambda<1/2`, the linear-chain theorem gives

\[
a_{z_0}=O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
a_{z_\ell}>\lambda D,
\]

and

\[
\ell\ge
\left({\lambda\over1+h_*}-o(1)\right)D.
\]

Combining with (OF.7), the outer face contains a polygonal chain of old fully inactive source points whose support-depth span is

\[
\boxed{\lambda D-O(\sqrt D).}
\tag{OF.8}
\]

The chain starts outside the force hull and penetrates linearly far inward in the module support coordinate without crossing the positive contact network.

For example, with `lambda=0.49`,

\[
\boxed{
\ell\ge(0.3671105001\ldots-o(1))D,
}
\tag{OF.9}

while the support-depth span exceeds

\[
0.49D-O(\sqrt D).
\]

As `lambda` tends to `1/2` from below, the chain-length coefficient approaches

\[
0.374602551093811\ldots .
\]

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The current reflection alternative can now be stated more sharply:

```text
EXACT-BRAID
    D^(1/4-o(1)) tagged actual source diameters
    pairwise crossing through one o(D) central core

or

OUTER-FACE INACTIVE CORRIDOR
    a selected FREE module starts outside the force hull
    one old-inactive near-unit chain remains in the outer contact face
    chain length Omega(D)
    support-depth penetration lambda D for every fixed lambda<1/2
```

Thus a non-anchored reflection module cannot hide in an internal exceptional chamber. Its repair obstruction is a macroscopic corridor in the outer contact face, beginning outside the actual force hull.

This points directly at the active canonical currency left in the promoted Target C route: the outer-face/outer-walk detour and its coupling to the actual diameter graph.

**Disposition:** proved `U cap F = emptyset` for every FREE module and upgraded the reflection-induced linear inactive chain to a macroscopic outer-face corridor. C-II remains open.
